From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: hand data frames to the NSS firmware

The firmware owns the transmit ring, so this driver cannot put a frame on
it, and the handover left transmit refused. A frame goes to the firmware
instead, addressed to the virtual device the firmware knows the interface
by, and a frame the firmware hands up names the same device.

The virtual device is registered once the interface's encapsulation mode
is set and released before the interface goes, so the firmware never names
one whose peers are already gone. Only an already-encapsulated frame is
handed over: the hardware builds the 802.11 header from an ethernet frame,
so a frame still carrying its own header is not one the firmware would read
the same way. Management leaves over the control path above this, unchanged.

The interface number the registration returns is what a transmitted frame
is addressed to, so it is kept with the interface rather than looked up per
frame.

The firmware returns a frame with no transmit status, and the control block
the subsystem reports against is overwritten on the way down. A frame that
asks for a status is reported unacknowledged at the divert and a clone is
sent instead, so its ack-status entry is released.

The firmware also hands up frames the hardware did not decapsulate, such
as a block ack request from a station, on the same path as ethernet. The
receive descriptor at the head of the buffer gives the format, and such a
frame goes to mac80211 as 802.11, as on the host receive path.

Received data frames no longer pass through mac80211, which took a
station's RX bitrate from them. The rate is taken from the monitor status
ring instead, which carries the preamble type and frame control once the
PPDU end user stats TLV is enabled; the last PPDU carrying data sets it.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
Index: backports-7.2/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.h
